{"type":"match","data":{"path":{"text":"www.bestcollegereviews.org/100 Most Beautiful College Campuses In America.txt"},"lines":{"text":"35. University of San Diego – San Diego, California\n"},"line_number":175,"absolute_offset":32725,"submatches":[{"match":{"text":"University of San Diego"},"start":4,"end":27}]}}
{"type":"match","data":{"path":{"text":"www.bestcollegereviews.org/100 Most Beautiful College Campuses In America.txt"},"lines":{"text":"It's worth taking a trip to the University of San Diego just to see the magnificent Immaculata Church, which adds both grandeur and beauty to the school's 180-acre campus. Constructed by the Diocese of San Diego and receiving its dedication in 1959, it's an architectural marvel, whatever your religious stripe. Particularly noteworthy are the church's opulent entrance – which boasts a door made out of solid bronze – and the Spanish-inspired touches everywhere from the red-tiled roof to the mosaicked dome. Many of the other buildings on the San Diego campus also nod to Europe, thanks to a Spanish Renaissance architectural style that harks back to the 16th century. The University of San Diego is one of the youngest schools on this list, having been established in 1949 as the San Diego College for Women; the institution then became co-ed in 1972.\n"},"line_number":177,"absolute_offset":32780,"submatches":[{"match":{"text":"University of San Diego"},"start":32,"end":55},{"match":{"text":"University of San Diego"},"start":679,"end":702}]}}
